			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1411055">
									

<p>The latest &quot;Visions of a better world&quot; brought this object to my attention.<br />
Containment procedures: &quot;having a network interface&quot; does not equal &quot;internet access&quot;.<br />
Second, what is the last time you've seen a T3 NIC suited for home PCs? Everything I managed to find is a Cisco specialized card for Cisco hardware.<br />
Third, T3 operates at 44 megabits per second. I don't know what medieval hardware would consider this speed &quot;immense&quot;.<br />
Fourth. A text file? Seriously? This is a network device, not a disk drive. Maybe a file share, HTTP or FTP server with these files.<br />
Fifth. How does it discern between 17 billions of different bacteria, 33 cockroaches, 4 people and a cactus in the room (see Drewbear's post earlier)?</p>
<p>That said, the idea is good, but the execution is flawed.</p>

