<p>1st; I still think that can be rephrased to something better. Your computer is perfectly capable of an internet connection, yet it does not have a T3 NIC. Hence &quot;&#8230;moved to any computer having an appropriate network interface&quot; may be a better phrase.<br />
2nd; OK, point taken.<br />
3rd; 44 megabits? Oh come on, the article was wrtitten 3 years ago. Gigabit ethernet came into being at least 8 years ago.<br />
4th; I object. This thing operates at OSI level 3. <em>The NIC at the computer it is connected to</em> operates at OSI level 3. Files belong to level 7. So, unless some serious SCP magic is happening <em>at the computer</em>, files are not what you should see. Also, in case of Linux-based network equipment you can telnet / ssh to them and change the configuration in-place, via files or commands, but you don't see the files right after you connect your PC to it.<br />
5th; Maybe. The latter makes the whole thing creepier.</p>

